           <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 bg-white p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-slate-100">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-brand-dark mb-3">Project Background</h4> <!--[cite: 2] -->
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-3">This case simulates a two-week youth camp project. 39 participants (ages 14-18, diverse genders and nationalities) rated their engagement (on a 1-5 scale) across 6 core activities at the end of Week 1 and Week 2. <!--[cite: 2] --></p>
                    <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-sm">Following Week 1, the project team made several micro-adjustments to the activity execution based on engagement feedback (e.g., adding a movie night voting option). This analysis focuses on the quantitative comparison between Week 1 and Week 2 to evaluate the overall trend in engagement changes, without attributing causality to specific adjustment strategies. <!--[cite: 2] --></p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-semibold text-brand-dark mb-3">Analysis Objectives</h4> <!--[cite: 2] -->
                    <ul class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed text-sm space-y-3">
                        <li class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-2">
                            <span class="text-slate-400 select-none">•</span>
                            <span><strong class="text-brand-dark font-semibold">Overall Trend:</strong> Did engagement significantly improve in Week 2 compared to Week 1? What was the magnitude of this change? <!--[cite: 2] --></span>
                        </li>
                        <li class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-2">
                            <span class="text-slate-400 select-none">•</span>
                            <span><strong class="text-brand-dark font-semibold">Individual Consistency:</strong> Is this improvement concentrated among a few participants, or is it a widespread trend across most individuals? <!--[cite: 2] --></span>
                        </li>
                        <li class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-2">
                            <span class="text-slate-400 select-none">•</span>
                            <span><strong class="text-brand-dark font-semibold">Statistical Reliability:</strong> Does the data meet the assumptions for a paired-samples t-test? Are the conclusions statistically robust? <!--[cite: 2] --></span>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
